{"pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Assignment 2 — Batch Growth Kinetics \u000aQ1. Growth from experimental data \u000aA microorganism is cultivated in a batch reactor. During exponential growth, the following \u000abiomass concentrations are measured: \u000a\u000aTime (h) Biomass X (g/L)\u000a\u000a2 0.80\u000a\u000a4 1.60\u000a\u000a6 3.20\u000a\u000aa. Calculate the specific growth rate, μ, between 2 and 6 h. \u000ab. Calculate the biomass doubling time. \u000ac. Would you expect the same μ to apply throughout the entire batch cultivation? Explain. \u000a\u000aQ2. Interpreting a batch-growth profile \u000aA batch culture initially contains 20 g/L glucose. Biomass increases rapidly between 2 and 8 h, \u000awhile glucose decreases. At approximately 10 h, glucose approaches zero and biomass \u000aconcentration stops increasing. \u000a\u000aAnswer the following: \u000a\u000aa. What growth phase is most likely occurring between 2 and 8 h? \u000ab. Why does biomass stop increasing at approximately 10 h? \u000ac. Does the cessation of net biomass accumulation necessarily mean that all cellular metabolic \u000aactivity has stopped? Explain. \u000a\u000aQ3. Biomass yield \u000aA batch fermentation starts with: \u000a\u000a• Biomass = 0.5 g/L \u000a• Glucose = 25 g/L \u000a\u000aAt the end of cultivation: \u000a• Biomass = 10.5 g/L \u000a• Glucose = 5 g/L \u000a\u000aa. Calculate the observed biomass yield coefficient,  . \u000ab. Explain biologically what this value means. \u000ac. Why should the initial biomass not simply be included as biomass produced from the substrate \u000aduring this batch? \u000a\u000a1","scan":false,"lines":[{"y":88.34,"x":72,"e":379.17,"s":18,"b":true,"i":false,"r":false,"f":"AAAAAB+TimesNewRomanPS-BoldMT","c":0,"sp":[[72,"Assignment 2 — Batch Growth Kinetics",18,[[72,"Assignment",1,0,0,162.01,0],[166.51,"2",1,0,0,175.51,0],[180.01,"—",1,0,0,198.01,0],[202.51,"Batch",1,0,0,247.51,0],[252.01,"Growth",1,0,0,311.68,0],[316.18,"Kinetics",1,0,0,379.17,0]]]]},{"